    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <ul class="faq-list">
      <li class="faq-item">
        <h3 class="faq-q">What is the difference between Growth and Balanced super options?</h3>
        <p class="faq-a">A Growth option typically holds 70–85% in shares and property and aims for higher long-run returns — often 8–9% per annum — with greater short-term volatility. A Balanced option holds 55–70% in growth assets with more bonds and cash for stability, targeting around 6–7.5% per annum. Growth is better suited to younger members with a long time horizon; Balanced suits those approaching mid-career or pre-retirement.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="faq-item">
        <h3 class="faq-q">Should I switch to Conservative before a recession?</h3>
        <p class="faq-a">Trying to time the market by switching to Conservative ahead of a downturn is extremely difficult to do successfully. If you switch to Conservative after markets have already fallen, you lock in your losses and miss the recovery. Unless you are genuinely close to retirement, staying in a Growth or Balanced option through downturns has historically produced better outcomes than switching defensively.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="faq-item">
        <h3 class="faq-q">At what age should I change my super investment option?</h3>
        <p class="faq-a">There is no single right age, but a commonly used guide is Growth until your early-to-mid 50s, transitioning toward Balanced in your late 50s, and shifting a portion toward Conservative or Cash within five years of your planned retirement. Your personal financial situation — current balance, other assets, health, retirement date — should drive the decision more than age alone.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="faq-item">
        <h3 class="faq-q">Can I split my super across multiple investment options?</h3>
        <p class="faq-a">Yes. Most super funds allow you to allocate your balance across multiple options — for example, 60% Growth and 40% Balanced. You can also direct future contributions to a different option than your existing balance. Log into your fund's online portal to check what splitting arrangements are available.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="faq-item">
        <h3 class="faq-q">Will switching super investment options trigger capital gains tax?</h3>
        <p class="faq-a">No. Switching investment options within your super fund is not a taxable event for the member. Investment earnings inside super are taxed at 15% at the fund level, but the act of switching between options does not create a CGT event. This is a key advantage of managing your investment mix inside super compared to a taxable investment account.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="faq-item">
        <h3 class="faq-q">How do I know which investment option my super is currently in?</h3>
        <p class="faq-a">Log in to your fund's online portal or member app. Your current investment option will be listed under "Investment Strategy" or "My Investments." If you have never made an active choice, you are most likely in your fund's default MySuper or Balanced option. Your annual member statement will also show your current option and the net return it delivered over the past year.</p>
      </li>
    </ul>

